{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive lineup change",
    "shot mix",
    "personnel effects",
    "within-game substitution",
    "play-type distribution"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "causal identification of lineup effects",
    "confounding by game situation in lineup change analysis",
    "reliability of play-type classification for shot mix",
    "within-game substitution design for detecting personnel effects",
    "sample size and power for shot-mix shift detection"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-007",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Teams run more cuts/PNR and fewer spot-ups/drives when a shaper is on the floor: shaper CUT +1.353pp, PNR +0.98pp, SPOT_UP -1.248pp, DRIVE -0.542pp. Within-team on/off analysis of play-type shares across 73 team-seasons."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF34",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "When opposing rim-protecting big is off the floor, offenses slightly increase drives (+0.82pp, t=+3.3) and decrease three‑point attempts (-1.79pp, t=-5.8), but rim share rises only +0.12pp (SE 0.295) and overall PPP +0.008 with t=+1.1; effects are small and sub-1 percentage point."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Lineup coverage (having at least one organizer, shaper, and terminal) varies within team, is separable from talent (|rho(COVERED,TALENT)|=0.042), and talent control behaves, based on 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-021",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Shot mix shifts by halftime margin, crunch time, and after runs. For example, crunch OTHER +6.08pp, PULL_UP +1.20pp at -0.045 PPP; post-run DRIVE +3.75pp, OTHER -6.72pp. No inferential tests reported, and OTHER label known to be noisy."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
      "what_failed": "play-type classifier producing biased shot-mix measures",
      "why": "Assisted flag existed only on made baskets, inflating SPOT_UP to 2.83 PPP; after rewrite SPOT_UP ~1.16 PPP. Demonstrates label error in possession-level play types."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-001",
      "what_failed": "adaptation falsification tests (asymmetry, confidence, carryover)",
      "why": "Wrong-way teams did not perform worse, confidence gradient absent, no carryover; labeled an artifact likely produced by selection/regression-to-the-mean and label noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "what_failed": "detecting defensive adaptation from PULL_UP exposure",
      "why": "Effect vanished without lineup fixed effects and matched the placebo for other play types, indicating shooter-level confounding and placebo dependence."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "inferring within-game defensive scheme changes from PBP",
      "why": "Inferred labels could not separate real defensive adjustments from offensive execution differences; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise; classified as untestable."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-017",
      "what_failed": "using team-season defensive PPP as a reliable adaptation trigger",
      "why": "Defensive PPP-allowed reliability 0.173–0.311 for key play types, too low for reliable triggering."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "When specific offensive player types (shapers) enter the game, within-team play-type mix shifts by detectable amounts (SF-PAP-007).",
    "Opponent lineup changes (removing a rim-protecting big) shift the offense's shot mix toward more drives and fewer threes, but the effects are small (SF34).",
    "Lineup coverage (presence of organizer/shaper/terminal) is a meaningful within-team variation that can be separated from overall talent (SF-PAP-009).",
    "Play-type classification has known measurement reliability problems, including a past data leak (SF-PAP-096) and low defensive PPP reliability (SF-TMP-017), which would weaken any shot-mix analysis.",
    "Shot mix does shift within games for tactical reasons (halftime, crunch, runs) unrelated to personnel changes (SF-APPM-021), indicating that confounding by game situation is a major concern."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether changing the entire offensive five (all five players) causes a measurable shift in shot mix beyond what is already expected from individual player effects.",
    "The size, direction, or robustness of a full-five substitution effect isolated from game situation, score, opponent lineup, and coach intent.",
    "That a design comparing shot mix before and after a full-five change can detect a personnel effect rather than a situational confound.",
    "The statistical power to detect a full-five shot-mix shift given the number of such events and the noise in play-type classification.",
    "The stability of shot-mix shifts across different team-seasons or opponent contexts."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question is likely intended as a methodological sanity check: if the analysis system cannot detect a shot-mix change when the entire offensive five changes, it lacks sensitivity. However, a detected change is expected and could arise from many factors (player skill profiles, fatigue, deliberate rest strategies), so a positive finding would not uniquely demonstrate the design's ability to detect personnel effects.",
    "Even if a raw before-vs-after comparison finds a shift, decomposing that shift into a genuine personnel effect versus a reaction to game context (score, opponent, period, rest) would require careful handling of confounding, likely through matched controls or natural-experiment designs (e.g., forced substitutions due to fouls or injuries).",
    "The play-type classification engine used in the project has undergone at least one significant rewrite (after SF-PAP-096), so the current data quality for shot-mix measures must be verified before analysis."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Exact play-type classification quality in the 2023-24 and 2024-25 datasets after the assisted-flag fix; some play types (like OTHER) may still be noisy.",
    "Number of 'five-man change' events per game and the possessions in the new lineup (exposure can be sparse for niche lineups).",
    "Effect size distribution across different offensive five changes; some may show large shifts, others none.",
    "Stability of shot mix within a single lineup stint (measurement noise vs. true tactical variation).",
    "Extent of confounding by the coach’s substitution strategy (e.g., resting starters when leading by 20, which would also change shot mix)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The retrieval revealed that a closely related analysis (individual player-type effect on shot mix) has already been supported (SF-PAP-007) and that opponent-based lineup effects on shot mix have been studied with small, inconclusive results (SF34). It also surfaced critical risks: play-type label errors (SF-PAP-096), regression-to-the-mean artifacts in adaptation studies (SF-APPA-001), and the confounding of tactical changes with personnel changes (SF-APPM-021). These findings shift the assessment from a simple sanity check toward a design requiring rigorous confounding controls, label-quality verification, and pre-registered falsification, leading to a 'proceed_with_changes' decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.55,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Lineup changes are not random; they occur in specific game situations (score, period, rest, opponent) that also influence shot mix. Isolating the personnel effect from these situational confounders requires a strong identification strategy."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Play-type classifications are known to have reliability issues and historical data leaks (SF-PAP-096). The current classifier may still misclassify certain shot types, diluting or biasing the measured mix."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Full five-man changes may provide only a handful of possessions for many lineups, limiting the ability to estimate stable shot-mix proportions for each specific five."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-PAP-007", "FAM:SF34", "FAM:SF-PAP-009"],
    "summary": "SF-PAP-007 shows that individual player archetypes shift play-type mix when on the floor. SF34 shows opponent lineup changes shift shot mix, but effects are small. SF-PAP-009 establishes that lineup coverage varies meaningfully within teams. None of these studies tested a full five-man substitution shot-mix shift."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Substitution timing is endogenous: coaches replace the five when the game situation (score, period, rest) is favorable, which independently affects shot selection."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Inferred play types may misclassify shots, especially for categories like OTHER, PULL_UP, and SPOT_UP, potentially masking or exaggerating mix changes."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "The play-type labels are algorithmically inferred and have a known history of systematic errors (SF-PAP-096). The post-fix classifier must be validated before it can support a shot-mix analysis."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Five-man changes may occur only a few times per game, and the number of possessions per stint may be small, limiting power to detect modest but meaningful shot-mix shifts."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Several play types would be examined simultaneously; without a global test or correction, false positives are likely."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Lineups are often changed after a poor stretch or cold shooting spell; the subsequent shot mix may regress to the mean, creating an apparent change that is not a personnel effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Shot-mix proportions are bounded (0–100%) and some play types have low base rates, causing floor/ceiling effects that can distort measurements."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.4,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.05,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.35
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal change in the offensive team's possession-level play-type probability vector (e.g., PNR, ISO, SPOT_UP, DRIVE, POST_UP, CUT, TRANSITION, OTHER) attributable to a change in the offensive five, while controlling for game state and opponent lineup.",
    "unit": "One offensive possession.",
    "sample": "All possessions from NBA 2023-24 and 2024-25 regular seasons (2022-23 and 2024-25 playoffs as robustness) meeting these criteria: (1) possessions in the first 42 minutes of regulation (exclude last 6 minutes to avoid intentional-foul and desperation situations); (2) excluding possessions after timeouts, free throws, and dead-ball turnovers (to reduce coach-called tactical changes); (3) full-five substitution events defined as a rotation interval where all five offensive players differ from the immediately preceding interval. For each such event, use the first 5 offensive possessions in the new five (to capture the 'fresh' shot mix) and compare to a matched control: the first 5 possessions of the same five when it returns later in the game (if any) or possessions from another game within the same season where the same five appeared with similar score differential (+/- 5 points) and period.",
    "power": "Monte Carlo simulation based on observed shot-mix variability (within-team, within-five standard deviations from SF-PAP-007 and SF34). Target: 80% power to detect a global chi-squared test departure of at least a 2-percentage-point shift in any single play type at alpha=0.05 (Bonferroni-corrected per-type). Given an estimated 3000 full-five changes per season with 5 possessions each, preliminary power is adequate for shifts of 1.5 pp or larger; smaller shifts may require pooling across team-seasons.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects: team-season, opponent-team, period (Q1–Q4), and a categorical variable for opponent lineup cluster (binned by presence of a rim-protecting big, primary ball-handler, etc.). Continuous controls: score differential (linear and quadratic), game time remaining. Also include the pre-change shot mix of the prior five to adjust for the immediate game context and regression-to-the-mean.",
    "falsification": "Placebo test 1: Apply the same pre-vs-post analysis to 'pseudo-changes'—points in the game where a full-five change could have occurred (same game time, score) but did not, and test for a shot-mix shift. Placebo test 2: If a new five appears multiple times, test whether the shot-mix shift is consistent across stints (reliability). Placebo test 3: Predict the expected shot mix of the new five from the individual player's average shot profiles and test whether the observed mix deviates systematically; if the observed shift is entirely predictable, the design is detecting composition effects (which is expected and not a detection failure).",
    "multiplicity": "Use a global likelihood-ratio test of the multinomial model comparing the treatment (new five) vs. control possessions, with play types as categories. If the global test rejects (alpha=0.05), then examine individual play-type differences with Holm-Bonferroni adjustment.",
    "decision_rule": "The design is deemed to 'detect a personnel effect' if the global test rejects and at least one play type shows a Holm-significant shift in the predicted direction (based on the individual player's historical shot tendencies). If the global test does not reject, we conclude the design cannot reliably detect the expected personnel effect at this sample size.",
    "data_version": "Post-rewrite play-type classification (after the assisted-flag leak fix documented in SF-PAP-096), generated from the graph database of play-by-play derived possessions, as of 2026-11-02."
  }
}}

